This is a greedy simulation, written without testing, so I can't confirm the scores.

**Approach**
- Keep an event-driven simulation with a current time `t`.
- Repeatedly pick the project with the best score potential that can be staffed right now with contributors free at time `t`.
- If nothing can be staffed, advance `t` to the next contributor free time.
- Stop when no project can ever be staffed or no contributors remain busy.

**Simplified version**
- Maintain a list of unscheduled projects, ordered by a priority key: the effective score at the earliest possible start, divided by duration and role count. Projects that would score zero are dropped.
- Loop over rounds. In each round, scan the projects in priority order and try to staff each one at time `t`, using only contributors whose free time is ≤ `t`. If staffing succeeds, schedule it.
- Because projects are scheduled in time order, contributors' skills at that moment are correct. A project started at `t` finishes at `t+D`, and the contributors' skills are updated immediately. This is safe because they are busy until `t+D`.
- Skill levels are updated at scheduling time. Another project scheduled later starts at a time ≥ `t+D` for those contributors, so this is consistent.
- A project is scheduled only when all its contributors are free at `t`, so it starts exactly at `t`. Output order is by scheduling order, which is non-decreasing in `t`, so the judge's simulation matches.

**Staffing a project**
- For each role in order, pick a free contributor not already used on this project, with level ≥ required, choosing the lowest level (minimising waste).
- If none qualifies, allow a contributor at level required−1, provided a mentor exists. A mentor is any team member, already chosen or to be chosen, with level ≥ required. To keep this simple, I check mentor availability against the already-chosen members plus a pre-pass: process roles in descending required level, then verify the mentoring condition at the end. If the final check fails, the staffing is rejected.
- Each contributor has a map from skill to level, and each skill has a list of contributors. The lowest-level search scans the skill's contributor list, which is acceptable for the time limit when capped at a number of scans per round.

**Efficiency**
- Each round scans the remaining projects, which could be 1e5 per round.
- To bound the time, I sort the projects once by priority and cap the number of rounds through a time check.
- I use the clock and stop at about 8 seconds.
- When a project fails to staff at time `t`, it is retried at later rounds. Each round advances `t` to the next distinct free time.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;
int main(){
    int C,P;
    scanf("%d %d",&C,&P);
    unordered_map<string,int> sid;
    vector<string> cn(C);
    vector<unordered_map<int,int>> sk(C);
    char b[64];
    auto gs=[&](const char*s){string x=s;auto it=sid.find(x);if(it!=sid.end())return it->second;int k=sid.size();sid[x]=k;return k;};
    for(int i=0;i<C;i++){
        int n;scanf("%s %d",b,&n);cn[i]=b;
        for(int j=0;j<n;j++){int l;scanf("%s %d",b,&l);sk[i][gs(b)]=l;}
    }
    vector<string> pn(P);
    vector<int> D(P),S(P),B(P);
    vector<vector<pair<int,int>>> R(P);
    for(int i=0;i<P;i++){
        int r;scanf("%s %d %d %d %d",b,&D[i],&S[i],&B[i],&r);pn[i]=b;
        for(int j=0;j<r;j++){int l;scanf("%s %d",b,&l);R[i].push_back({gs(b),l});}
    }
    vector<int> freeAt(C,0);
    vector<int> order(P);
    iota(order.begin(),order.end(),0);
    sort(order.begin(),order.end(),[&](int a,int c){
        double x=(double)S[a]/D[a]/R[a].size(),y=(double)S[c]/D[c]/R[c].size();
        return x>y;});
    vector<int> rem=order;
    vector<pair<int,vector<int>>> out;
    long long t=0;
    auto st=chrono::steady_clock::now();
    vector<char> used(C,0);
    while(!rem.empty()){
        if(chrono::duration<double>(chrono::steady_clock::now()-st).count()>7.5)break;
        vector<int> nrem;
        for(int p:rem){
            long long end=t+D[p];
            if(S[p]-max(0LL,end-B[p])<=0)continue;
            int r=R[p].size();
            vector<int> asg(r,-1);
            vector<int> ro(r);iota(ro.begin(),ro.end(),0);
            sort(ro.begin(),ro.end(),[&](int a,int c){return R[p][a].second>R[p][c].second;});
            vector<int> team;bool ok=true;
            auto lvl=[&](int c,int s){auto it=sk[c].find(s);return it==sk[c].end()?0:it->second;};
            for(int k:ro){
                int s=R[p][k].first,l=R[p][k].second;
                int best=-1,bl=1e9;
                bool mentor=false;
                for(int c:team)if(lvl(c,s)>=l)mentor=true;
                for(int c=0;c<C;c++){
                    if(freeAt[c]>t||used[c])continue;
                    int v=lvl(c,s);
                    if(v>=l||(v==l-1&&mentor)){
                        if(v<bl){bl=v;best=c;}
                    }
                }
                if(best<0){ok=false;break;}
                used[best]=1;team.push_back(best);asg[k]=best;
            }
            if(ok){
                // verify mentoring
                for(int k=0;k<r&&ok;k++){
                    int s=R[p][k].first,l=R[p][k].second;
                    if(lvl(asg[k],s)<l){
                        bool m=false;
                        for(int j=0;j<r;j++)if(j!=k&&lvl(asg[j],s)>=l)m=true;
                        if(!m)ok=false;
                    }
                }
            }
            for(int c:team)used[c]=0;
            if(!ok){nrem.push_back(p);continue;}
            for(int k=0;k<r;k++){
                int c=asg[k],s=R[p][k].first,l=R[p][k].second;
                if(lvl(c,s)<=l)sk[c][s]=lvl(c,s)+1;
                freeAt[c]=end;
            }
            out.push_back({p,asg});
        }
        rem=nrem;
        long long nt=LLONG_MAX;
        for(int c=0;c<C;c++)if(freeAt[c]>t)nt=min(nt,(long long)freeAt[c]);
        if(nt==LLONG_MAX)break;
        t=nt;
    }
    printf("%d\n",(int)out.size());
    for(auto&o:out){
        printf("%s\n",pn[o.first].c_str());
        for(size_t i=0;i<o.second.size();i++)printf("%s%s",i?" ":"",cn[o.second[i]].c_str());
        printf("\n");
    }
}
```

**Known weaknesses**
- The per-role scan over all contributors is O(C) per role, which can be slow on large inputs. The 7.5 s time guard prevents timeouts, but may cut off scheduling early.
- The skill level update uses `lvl ≤ l`, matching the statement's rule that levels increase when the required level is equal or higher.
